Congestive Heart Failure (Right-Sided)
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Fluid Overload
Elevated jugular venous pressure (JVP), Tender hepatomegaly, Significant dependent pitting edema

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-013
Reason for Visit: Worsening severe leg swelling, abdominal fullness, and profound fatigue, requiring urgent after-hours evaluation for acute fluid overload and impending right ventricular decompensation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult son
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted rapidly progressive lower limb swelling extending to the thighs, accompanied by abdominal pain and an inability to eat. This presentation necessitated an urgent assessment to evaluate for severe systemic venous congestion and right-sided heart failure that could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Massive leg swelling, stomach pain, and exhaustion. Patient states, My legs are like tree trunks, my stomach feels incredibly tight, and I get out of breath just walking to the kitchen.
History of Present Illness: Over the past two weeks, the patient has noted a gradual but relentless accumulation of significant bilateral pitting pedal and pretibial edema that has now extended proximally to the mid-thighs. Over the last 48 hours, the patient reports new onset of early satiety, anorexia, and abdominal discomfort secondary to bowel and hepatic congestion. Ascites has resulted in noticeably increased abdominal girth, making clothes unwearable. The patient describes profound fatigue and exertional intolerance, limiting mobility completely. The son reports the patient has been predominantly bedbound for the last two days due to the sheer weight of the legs.
Functional Status: Largely bedbound and chairbound; highly dependent on son for daily activities.
Social/Safety Concerns: Fall risk due to altered center of gravity and heavy limbs; son is available for support.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/75 mmHg | HR: 102 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 94.0 Kg (Reported baseline 86.0 Kg)
General: Appears chronically unwell, fatigued, sitting upright in a recliner, with visibly massive lower limbs.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia with a regular rhythm. The diagnostic triad is overtly present: assessment reveals a markedly elevated jugular venous pressure (JVP) visible 6 cm above the sternal angle, indicating severe systemic venous hypertension. Abdominal palpation reveals tender hepatomegaly extending 4 cm below the right costal margin. A positive hepatojugular reflux is elicited. Respiratory: Lungs are surprisingly clear to auscultation, lacking the overt fine crackles of left-sided failure. Gastrointestinal/Extremities: Ascites is demonstrable with a positive fluid thrill. There is significant dependent pitting edema (3+) extending bilaterally up to the sacrum, given the patient's recently bedbound status.
Environmental Observations: Ground-level living. Patient is positioned safely in an electric recliner chair.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient reports poor compliance with oral Furosemide over the past fortnight.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Congestive Heart Failure (Right-Sided) - Status: Decompensated
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The swelling is profoundly bilateral and symmetrical, accompanied by systemic signs of venous congestion (JVP, hepatomegaly) rather than an isolated, unilateral limb occlusion.

Liver Cirrhosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a markedly elevated JVP and positive hepatojugular reflux, which definitively point to a cardiac origin for the systemic congestion rather than primary hepatic portal hypertension.

Nephrotic Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Tender pulsatile hepatomegaly and elevated JVP, which are central cardiac signs absent in primary renal protein-losing states.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe systemic fluid overload strictly characteristic of right-sided congestive heart failure. The gross accumulation of fluid in the dependent limbs, abdomen, and liver indicates a critical inability of the right ventricle to accommodate venous return. The massive weight gain (estimated 8kg of fluid) and resulting anorexia due to gut edema placed the patient at high risk for further rapid hemodynamic compromise and malnutrition. Urgent after-hours assessment was definitively required to confirm the diagnosis, rule out concurrent left-sided pulmonary edema, and initiate an immediate, high-dose oral diuretic strategy to offload the right ventricle, bridging the patient safely until an urgent morning specialist review. The expected investigation results include right ventricular dilation, hypertrophy, or reduced ejection fraction on transthoracic echocardiography that would assist in confirming the diagnosis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered an immediate stat dose of oral Furosemide (80mg) to initiate brisk diuresis. Implemented strict fluid restriction (1.2L/day) and a low-sodium diet.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage your fluid weight safely at home, commit to weighing yourself every single morning immediately after emptying your bladder. Record this number in a specific diary. When you see the numbers dropping daily after taking your water pill, internally validate that your heart is successfully offloading the dangerous fluid. This visual proof of your progress will strongly reinforce your compliance with daily weighing and medication routines.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to Community Nursing for daily weight monitoring and medication administration support.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review tomorrow morning to assess initial diuretic response and check renal function (UEC).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call an ambulance immediately if you develop sudden, severe shortness of breath, if you begin coughing up pink frothy sputum, or if you experience crushing chest pain.

